{"items":[{"owner":{"account_id":30228,"reputation":216456,"user_id":82294,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":67,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/5c5f910416e2b92bb73fa59c56fe695d?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Dietrich Epp","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/82294/dietrich-epp"},"is_accepted":false,"score":24,"last_activity_date":1461979193,"last_edit_date":1461979193,"creation_date":1461979090,"answer_id":36949689,"question_id":36949665,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","body":"<p>I see a difference.</p>\n\n<p>First of all, what is up with <code>map(lambda x: x, st)</code>?  What is the purpose?</p>\n\n<p>Use the <code>timeit</code> module to test your code:</p>\n\n<pre><code>$ python -m timeit '\"\".join(reversed(\"abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz\"))'\n1000000 loops, best of 3: 0.586 usec per loop\n$ python -m timeit '\"abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz\"[::-1]'           \n10000000 loops, best of 3: 0.0715 usec per loop\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>As you can see, the slice is ~8x faster on my machine for this particular input.  It's also more concise.</p>\n"},{"owner":{"account_id":14262484,"reputation":53,"user_id":10302767,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/469cc67f2545fe4bf321776936e6367a?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Dilip kumar","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/10302767/dilip-kumar"},"is_accepted":false,"score":1,"last_activity_date":1542356851,"creation_date":1542356851,"answer_id":53334012,"question_id":36949665,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<pre><code>s=input(\"enter string\")\nprint(s[::-1])\n</code></pre>\n"}],"has_more":false,"quota_max":300,"quota_remaining":285}